00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:35.120
of the cloud [? Bigtable. ?]

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:37.850
So open source is
a big focus for us.

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:40.340
Now talking about
open source databases,

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:43.200
Redis, which is the topic
of today's conversation,

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:45.350
is one of the most
popular open source

00:05:45.350 --> 00:05:47.540
databases available today.

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:52.020
Now why is Redis such
a popular database?

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:54.320
So from my perspective,
the way I look at Redis

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:58.260
is that Redis is a very
versatile in-memory database.

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:00.770
So when I talk about
versatility of Redis,

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:04.100
Redis actually can be used to
solve a number of different use

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:04.980
cases.

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:08.510
For example, Redis-- a lot
of customers in, for example,

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:11.420
in Google Cloud today use
Redis for simple caching.

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:13.280
So when I talk about
caching, it could

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:15.530
be as simple as caching
some database queries,

00:06:15.530 --> 00:06:20.060
or it could be caching
some API results.

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:21.897
The other use cases
for Redis are typically

00:06:21.897 --> 00:06:23.480
our session management,
where you just

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:26.090
want to store a session token.

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:29.690
So when customers first start
off with Redis, for example,

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:32.900
they typically use
it as a simple cache.

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:35.870
But the versatility of
Redis comes from the fact

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:39.440
that it is actually a
data structure server.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:42.440
What I mean by that is
Redis provides a number

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:44.450
of powerful data
structures that allows

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:48.830
you to solve much bigger use
cases than simple caching.

00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:51.540
A very good example is
gaming leaderboards,

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:54.800
so with sorted sets in
Redis, it is very easy

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:59.100
to build leaderboards
for gaming applications.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:01.100
Similarly, for stream
processing or if you're

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:05.060
building IoT services, Redis
provides a number of great data

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:08.360
structures in order to do that.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:09.590
So if you look at how--

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:12.880
an example of how
Redis gets used,

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:14.720
one of the examples
that we commonly see

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:16.320
is stream processing.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:18.920
So in Google Cloud,
we see customers

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:21.770
using stream processing
for solving quite a number

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:24.120
of different use cases.

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:26.720
This is a very typical
example that we

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:31.550
see where customers use
Cloud Pub/Sub for basically

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:35.229
ingesting events that
they want to process.

00:07:35.229 --> 00:07:36.770
So a very good
example is that if you

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:39.470
want to process credit
card transactions,

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:42.290
and you want to be able
to make decisions on that,

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:46.880
you can use Cloud Pub/Sub,
which essentially is basically

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:49.970
an event streaming engine that
allows you to ingest events

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:51.510
pretty quickly.

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:54.170
And then you can use
Cloud Data Flow in order

00:07:54.170 --> 00:07:58.254
to process these events
very, very quickly.

00:07:58.254 --> 00:07:59.670
One of the key use
cases for Redis

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:02.150
that we see in this
particular architecture

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:06.440
is that customers use Redis
to basically store states

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:09.440
that they want to look
up really quickly,

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:12.170
or if they want to store some
aggregate data that they really

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:15.180
want to retrieve for
their processing.

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:18.680
So this is a very good
example of how Redis gets used

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:21.620
for stream processing use case.

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:23.420
So one of the key
things that customers

00:08:23.420 --> 00:08:25.790
have been asking from
Google Cloud Platform

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:29.030
is that they want to be able to
have a managed Redis service.

00:08:29.030 --> 00:08:31.670
So until now, customers have
been deploying their own Redis

00:08:31.670 --> 00:08:35.240
instance, and they've been
managing it themselves.

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:40.090
What I'm happy to say is that
we now have Cloud Memorystore.

00:08:40.090 --> 00:08:42.400
Cloud Memorystore, which
is currently in beta,

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:46.910
is a fully managed in-memory
datastore service for Redis.

00:08:46.910 --> 00:08:50.150
So what I want to do for
the rest of the presentation

00:08:50.150 --> 00:08:52.220
is to walk you through
Cloud Memorystore

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:54.940
and walk you through the
capabilities that we provide.

00:08:54.940 --> 00:09:00.060
Like I mentioned, this
service is currently in beta.

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:04.470
So let's take a look at what
Cloud Memorystore provides.

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:06.870
So one of the key
principles that'd

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:08.850
be followed for
Cloud Memorystore

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:12.610
is to make sure that the
service is fast, scalable,

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:14.310
highly available, and secure.

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:16.200
And more importantly,
we wanted to make sure

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:18.990
that you are focused on
building applications,

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:22.360
and we can take care of
just managing Redis for you.

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:23.820
And that's essentially
what we are

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:25.840
doing with Cloud Memorystore.

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:30.090
So with the Memorystore, it's
a fully managed Redis service.

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:35.070
One of the key things that we
provide is high availability.

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:38.640
So typically, when you
look at caching services,

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:41.850
you normally don't SLAs
associated with it.

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:45.480
With Cloud Memorystore, we
provide three nines of SLAs

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:47.650
for one of the tiers.

00:09:47.650 --> 00:09:51.570
And finally, the
Memorystore is obviously

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:54.750
built on Google's
scalable infrastructure.

00:09:54.750 --> 00:09:57.240
We provide a high
level of security

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:00.120
based on both the
networking infrastructure

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:03.910
and also using role-based
access control.

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:06.570
So I'll talk more about
these features in detail

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:08.320
as we move forward.

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:10.560
But I just want to
quickly point out

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:15.800
that with Cloud Memorystore
available in beta,

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:19.000
it's going to be very
easy to use Redis

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:21.270
to [INAUDIBLE] your
application, because now you

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:26.820
don't have to manage your own
Redis in Google Cloud Platform.

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:31.050
So let's take a look at
some of the features that

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:34.841
are available with
Cloud Memorystore today.

00:10:34.841 --> 00:10:36.340
The first thing I
want to touch upon

00:10:36.340 --> 00:10:38.340
is how we expose a service.

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:41.580
So Cloud Memorystore provides
two tiers of service--

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:44.260
a basic tier and
a standard tier.

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:45.400
So what is a basic tier?

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:49.120
So basic tier, basically,
is a single Redis instance.

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:51.430
Where we see customers
use basic tier

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:56.440
is when customers want to
use Redis as a simple cache.

00:10:56.440 --> 00:10:57.970
In this particular
case, application

00:10:57.970 --> 00:11:02.470
can tolerate the Redis
instance losing the data,

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:07.150
and they simply want
to have a cache that

00:11:07.150 --> 00:11:11.140
is actually cheaper in cost.

00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:12.730
The key thing about
the basic tier

00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:15.182
is that we do the
instance monitoring.

00:11:15.182 --> 00:11:17.140
So what that means is
that if something happens

00:11:17.140 --> 00:11:19.690
to the instance, we have
health monitoring in place that

00:11:19.690 --> 00:11:23.170
will basically take care
of restarting the instance

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:25.780
and recovering the
instance from failure.

00:11:25.780 --> 00:11:27.700
When you recover the
instance from failure,

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:29.590
obviously, the IP
address is maintained

00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:31.480
so that the
applications can simply

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:35.740
reconnect without any changes
on the application side.

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:37.860
One of the key things
about the basic tier that I

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:39.610
want to point out is
that we don't provide

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:42.440
an SLA for the basic tier.

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:43.970
So the standard
tier is essentially

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:49.850
what you want to use if you
rely on Redis as a key component

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:51.290
in your application,
and you want

00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:53.030
high availability for Redis.

00:11:53.030 --> 00:11:55.250
So what do we do in
the standard tier?

00:11:55.250 --> 00:11:58.760
We provide a replicated
instance of Redis.

00:11:58.760 --> 00:12:01.520
We provide one
secondary replica, which

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:03.410
is deployed at cross-zones.

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:06.470
So what that means is that
if there is a zonal failure,

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:10.940
then your applications
are unaffected,

00:12:10.940 --> 00:12:13.280
because we automatically
failover the Redis instance

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:16.310
over to the other zone.

00:12:16.310 --> 00:12:19.490
The other thing that we
provide is a seamless scale-up

00:12:19.490 --> 00:12:20.750
of capacity.

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:22.324
This is also available
in basic tier,

00:12:22.324 --> 00:12:24.740
but the key difference here
is that in standard tier, when

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:28.730
we do the scaling up the
instance up and down,

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:30.980
we provide high availability
for the instance.

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:34.460
And I'll talk more about
it when I show the demo.

00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:36.680
And lastly, like I mentioned,
with standard tier,

00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:38.900
you get three nines
of availability.

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:43.880
I want to note that the
availability SLA is applicable

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:45.860
when we go GA.

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:51.530
It's not applicable in
the current beta release.

00:12:51.530 --> 00:12:53.690
OK, so the other thing
that I want to touch upon

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:57.380
is apart from the
tiers, we also provide

00:12:57.380 --> 00:12:59.330
what we call capacity tiers.

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:03.770
So the way we expose
the servers is

00:13:03.770 --> 00:13:07.130
the more memory you provision,
the more throughput you get.

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:09.830
So as it can see in the
table, if you provision up

00:13:09.830 --> 00:13:14.900
to 35 gigabytes on Redis, you
get about three bits per second

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:16.610
in terms of network throughput.

00:13:16.610 --> 00:13:19.470
And if you provision greater
than 100 gigabytes of Redis,

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:22.920
you get 12 gigabits per
second network throughput.

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:26.870
So the key thing to note here
is that when you plan your Redis

00:13:26.870 --> 00:13:29.180
deployment using
Memorystore, you

00:13:29.180 --> 00:13:31.280
want to consider what your
application throughput

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:32.440
characteristics are.

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:34.910
And depending on that,
slice your Redis instance

00:13:34.910 --> 00:13:36.450
appropriately.

00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:38.150
One of the key
aspects of Redis is

00:13:38.150 --> 00:13:43.220
that Redis is single threaded,
so you always will have--

00:13:43.220 --> 00:13:45.830
in most cases, you
have sufficient CPU

00:13:45.830 --> 00:13:47.882
for these instances.

00:13:47.882 --> 00:13:49.340
The key thing that
you want to note

00:13:49.340 --> 00:13:51.590
is how much network
throughput you

00:13:51.590 --> 00:13:57.410
want to have before the
application saturates a Redis

00:13:57.410 --> 00:13:59.360
server.

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:04.370
So the capacity tier basically
tells you the network

00:14:04.370 --> 00:14:05.810
performance that you get.

00:14:05.810 --> 00:14:09.920
It also determines the
pricing of the Redis instance.

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:12.170
So the way we have
priced Memorystore

00:14:12.170 --> 00:14:17.280
is by dollar per gigabyte hour.

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:18.440
So what does that mean?

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:22.760
So if you're provisioning one
to four gigabytes of Redis,

00:14:22.760 --> 00:14:25.910
so anything less than or
equal to four gigabytes,

00:14:25.910 --> 00:14:28.730
you will be charged at
4.9 cents per gigabyte.

00:14:28.730 --> 00:14:33.230
Or similarly, as you go higher
up in the capacity range,

00:14:33.230 --> 00:14:35.420
obviously, your gigabyte
[? hour ?] pricing

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:39.340
is actually going to be lower.

00:14:39.340 --> 00:14:41.090
Standard tier
instance, similarly,

00:14:41.090 --> 00:14:45.110
are priced using the
same capacity ranges,

00:14:45.110 --> 00:14:48.661
but the pricing is going to be
more compared to the basic tier

00:14:48.661 --> 00:14:49.160
instance.

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:56.070
Like I said, Cloud Memorystore
is a managed service,

00:14:56.070 --> 00:14:58.660
so we make this provisioning
extremely simple.

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:00.870
We allow you to provision
Memorystore Redis

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:03.600
using Cloud Console
or Command Line

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:05.312
or using Google
Client Libraries.

00:15:05.312 --> 00:15:07.020
And the other key
thing that we have done

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:08.640
is we have integrated
with Terraform,

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:10.680
so if you are managing
your infrastructure

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:13.890
as infrastructure
as code, you can

00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:17.076
use Terraform to provision
the Redis instance.

00:15:17.076 --> 00:15:18.450
One key thing I
want to point out

00:15:18.450 --> 00:15:24.000
is that we are supporting
Redis version 3.2.11.

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:27.330
We will be looking at
supporting more versions later,

00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:34.272
but currently in beta, what
is available is 3.2.11.

00:15:34.272 --> 00:15:36.230
Just want to quickly
touch on high availability

00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:37.342
and what we do there.

00:15:37.342 --> 00:15:39.050
So the key thing I
want to point out here

00:15:39.050 --> 00:15:40.580
about high availability
architecture

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:42.420
or when you deploy a
standard tier instance

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:46.520
is that you are
connecting to a single IP,

00:15:46.520 --> 00:15:50.240
and, basically, we take
care of the redirection

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:54.980
of the application
connections to the right VMs

00:15:54.980 --> 00:15:56.670
if a failover happens.

00:15:56.670 --> 00:15:59.810
The other thing is that we
provide automatic failovers,

00:15:59.810 --> 00:16:01.340
so we have our
own health monitor

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:03.180
that monitors the instance.

00:16:03.180 --> 00:16:05.130
We monitor the health
of the processes,

00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:07.560
and if anything goes wrong,
we automatically failover.

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:10.170
And the failover time is
actually very, very fast.

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:11.760
It's actually in the
order of seconds.

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:14.300
So in terms of
application availability,

00:16:14.300 --> 00:16:16.270
you get pretty good
at high availability

00:16:16.270 --> 00:16:19.350
with a standard tier instance.

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:24.650
And finally, security--
the key thing

00:16:24.650 --> 00:16:27.020
that we try to do
with Cloud Memorystore

00:16:27.020 --> 00:16:30.290
is to protect the instance
from external attacks.

00:16:30.290 --> 00:16:31.730
So when we provision
the instance,

00:16:31.730 --> 00:16:34.040
we provision it
using private IP.

00:16:34.040 --> 00:16:37.610
So when you provision a
Cloud Memorystore instance,

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:40.700
it's not accessible
from the internet,

00:16:40.700 --> 00:16:44.340
because it is provisioned
using a private IP address.

00:16:44.340 --> 00:16:46.010
The other key
thing you can do is

00:16:46.010 --> 00:16:49.130
to limit the access of the
instance using Virtual Private

00:16:49.130 --> 00:16:50.050
Network.

00:16:50.050 --> 00:16:53.780
And we also support Cloud
Identity and Management roles,

00:16:53.780 --> 00:16:56.240
so we have granular
IAM roles that

00:16:56.240 --> 00:16:58.970
allows you to control
access to the Redis instance

00:16:58.970 --> 00:17:01.919
based on your
organizational rules.

00:17:01.919 --> 00:17:03.960
And the other key thing
that I want to point out,

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:06.290
which actually became
relevant very recently,

00:17:06.290 --> 00:17:08.060
is critical patch management.

00:17:08.060 --> 00:17:12.020
So Redis recently
announced a critical patch.

00:17:12.020 --> 00:17:14.240
We actually applied the
patch even before it

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:17.150
was publicly announced, so
one thing you can be sure of

00:17:17.150 --> 00:17:19.849
is that your Redis instances
or Cloud Memorystore

00:17:19.849 --> 00:17:21.829
instances are always
secure, because we

00:17:21.829 --> 00:17:24.170
take care of the
patching, and you

00:17:24.170 --> 00:17:27.540
don't have to worry about it.

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:30.350
And finally, I want to touch
upon some of the other features

00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:31.670
and capabilities.

00:17:31.670 --> 00:17:33.920
I talked about
scaling seamlessly.

00:17:33.920 --> 00:17:38.125
So we allow scaling of
the Memorystore instance

00:17:38.125 --> 00:17:40.310
at both basic and standard tier.

00:17:40.310 --> 00:17:42.680
You can scale your
memory up and down.

00:17:42.680 --> 00:17:44.960
With basic tier,
obviously, you will

00:17:44.960 --> 00:17:48.630
have your cache [INAUDIBLE],,
because its a single instance.

00:17:48.630 --> 00:17:51.200
But the standard tier of
scaling is a highly available

00:17:51.200 --> 00:17:52.560
operation.

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:54.950
We have integration
with Stackdriver,

00:17:54.950 --> 00:17:57.110
and I'll talk more
about it in the demo.

00:17:57.110 --> 00:17:59.750
And we also will be
providing the capability

00:17:59.750 --> 00:18:02.420
to back up your data
using RDB snapshots, which

00:18:02.420 --> 00:18:04.170
will be coming soon.

00:18:04.170 --> 00:18:08.090
The RDB snapshots is
not currently available

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:12.240
in beta today, but we plan to
make it available pretty soon.

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:15.560
And lastly, because we are
built on open source Redis,

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:16.760
we are protocol-compatible.

00:18:16.760 --> 00:18:19.218
So what that means is that if
you're running your own Redis

00:18:19.218 --> 00:18:22.460
instance today, you can switch
over to Memorystore instance

00:18:22.460 --> 00:18:25.430
with very little or
actually no code change,

00:18:25.430 --> 00:18:28.740
and everything should just work.

00:18:28.740 --> 00:18:31.590
OK, so that's kind of the
summary of the capabilities

00:18:31.590 --> 00:18:33.690
that we provide with
Cloud Memorystore.

00:18:33.690 --> 00:18:38.340
So let me quickly
switch over to the demo

00:18:38.340 --> 00:18:42.180
and show you how easy it
is to deploy and manage

00:18:42.180 --> 00:18:45.900
Cloud Memorystore.

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:48.770
So to get started, you go
to the Google Cloud Console,

00:18:48.770 --> 00:18:52.940
and Memorystore is a storage
and database service.

00:18:52.940 --> 00:18:54.860
So if you click on
Cloud Memorystore,

00:18:54.860 --> 00:18:58.790
it'll take you to the
Memorystore landing page.

00:18:58.790 --> 00:19:00.800
One of the key things I
want to point out here

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:03.680
is that if you're provisioning
Memorystore for the first time,

00:19:03.680 --> 00:19:06.360
we automatically
enable the API for you.

00:19:06.360 --> 00:19:08.360
And once the API is
enabled, you can start

00:19:08.360 --> 00:19:10.040
provisioning for instance.

00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:13.072
In this particular case, I have
provisioned instances already.

00:19:13.072 --> 00:19:14.780
But let's go ahead
and create an instance

00:19:14.780 --> 00:19:18.640
and see how easy
it is to do this.

00:19:18.640 --> 00:19:20.160
So when you provision
an instance,

00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:23.550
the first thing that you want to
do is give it an instance name.

00:19:23.550 --> 00:19:25.650
I'm just going to give it--

00:19:25.650 --> 00:19:28.950
let's say, Demo Instance
2, as I mentioned.

00:19:28.950 --> 00:19:30.390
The Redis version
that we provide

00:19:30.390 --> 00:19:32.430
is 3.2, so you
don't have an option

00:19:32.430 --> 00:19:34.650
to select a version
in the beta release,

00:19:34.650 --> 00:19:39.260
but we will be providing support
for more versions later on.

00:19:39.260 --> 00:19:42.090
And the next choice
that you want to make

00:19:42.090 --> 00:19:45.207
is whether it's a basic or
a standard tier instance.

00:19:45.207 --> 00:19:47.415
Like I mentioned earlier,
basic tier is a standalone,

00:19:47.415 --> 00:19:50.484
and standard tier is
a replicated instance.

00:19:50.484 --> 00:19:51.900
In this particular
case, I'm going

00:19:51.900 --> 00:19:54.870
to choose standard,
because I want to deploy

00:19:54.870 --> 00:19:56.784
a highly available instance.

00:19:56.784 --> 00:19:58.950
And the next thing I want
to do is select the region

00:19:58.950 --> 00:20:01.630
where you want to
deploy the instance.

00:20:01.630 --> 00:20:03.480
So the key thing I
want to point out here

00:20:03.480 --> 00:20:05.430
is that if you
choose zone as any,

00:20:05.430 --> 00:20:07.470
we automatically pick
the zones for you

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:10.050
for both the master
and the replica.

00:20:10.050 --> 00:20:13.800
But if you want to specify the
zone where the master is going

00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:16.740
to be deployed at
the time of creation,

00:20:16.740 --> 00:20:20.430
then you can go ahead and
select a specific zone

00:20:20.430 --> 00:20:23.220
where you want to deploy
the master instance.

00:20:23.220 --> 00:20:25.380
But even if you select
a zone for the master,

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:27.990
we will automatically
select a zone

00:20:27.990 --> 00:20:30.450
for the replica, which is
different from the zone

00:20:30.450 --> 00:20:31.680
that you selected.

00:20:31.680 --> 00:20:34.960
In this particular case, I'm
just going to leave it at any.

00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:37.530
And over here, this
is where you select

00:20:37.530 --> 00:20:39.480
the size of the Redis instance.

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:43.110
As I mentioned, if you change
the size of the instance,

00:20:43.110 --> 00:20:45.700
you basically get different
network throughput.

00:20:45.700 --> 00:20:49.560
So you want to be able to
determine what is the network

00:20:49.560 --> 00:20:52.230
throughput that you want to
get out of your Redis instance,

00:20:52.230 --> 00:20:55.050
and size the
instance accordingly.

00:20:55.050 --> 00:20:57.870
And lastly, you want
to pick the network

00:20:57.870 --> 00:21:00.030
that this Redis
instance is going

00:21:00.030 --> 00:21:02.430
to be authorized to connect,
so what that means is

00:21:02.430 --> 00:21:06.776
that VMs connected
to the VPC network

00:21:06.776 --> 00:21:08.400
where the Redis
instance is provisioned

00:21:08.400 --> 00:21:13.160
can actually access this
particular Redis instance.

00:21:13.160 --> 00:21:16.460
So let's go ahead and
create the instance.

00:21:16.460 --> 00:21:18.950
So as you can see, it
is actually very simple

00:21:18.950 --> 00:21:21.395
to go ahead and create this
instance-- create the Redis

00:21:21.395 --> 00:21:23.647
instance in using Memorystore.

00:21:23.647 --> 00:21:25.230
One quick thing I
do want to point out

00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:28.090
is that I talked about scaling.

00:21:28.090 --> 00:21:31.220
So I already provisioned
a basic instance here,

00:21:31.220 --> 00:21:33.670
and it is currently
one gigabyte.

00:21:33.670 --> 00:21:36.720
Now I want to go ahead and
change that to, let's say,

00:21:36.720 --> 00:21:38.180
10 gigabytes.

00:21:38.180 --> 00:21:39.530
As you can see, it's super easy.

00:21:39.530 --> 00:21:41.490
All I have to do is
go to Edit Instance

00:21:41.490 --> 00:21:45.600
and change the size of the
instance, and then click Save,

00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:49.260
and we automatically
do the scaling for you.

00:21:49.260 --> 00:21:51.840
As I mentioned earlier, when
you scale a basic tier instance,

00:21:51.840 --> 00:21:54.660
we recreate the instance, so
it's a full flush of the cache,

00:21:54.660 --> 00:21:59.250
but we preserve the IP address
so that applications can retry

00:21:59.250 --> 00:22:03.530
and connect back to
the same IP address.

00:22:03.530 --> 00:22:08.750
OK, so now I talked about
Redis getting provisioned

00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:10.560
on a private IP address.

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:16.820
As you can see, the IP that
we currently use for the demo

00:22:16.820 --> 00:22:19.892
instance is 10.0.0.4.

00:22:19.892 --> 00:22:21.350
So from a connectivity
perspective,

00:22:21.350 --> 00:22:23.040
there are a few things
to keep in mind.

00:22:23.040 --> 00:22:26.300
One is you can only connect to
the Memorystore Redis instance

00:22:26.300 --> 00:22:28.189
from within the
same region where

00:22:28.189 --> 00:22:29.480
you have deployed the instance.

00:22:29.480 --> 00:22:32.090
So that means you can
connect-- your application has

00:22:32.090 --> 00:22:33.890
to be running in
US [? Central ?] 1

00:22:33.890 --> 00:22:37.580
to be able to connect to this
particular Redis instance.

00:22:37.580 --> 00:22:43.880
Secondly, you cannot access
the Redis instance outside

00:22:43.880 --> 00:22:46.010
of the region, obviously.

00:22:46.010 --> 00:22:48.911
So for example, if you're
connecting from your laptop

00:22:48.911 --> 00:22:51.410
or trying to connect from your
laptop to the Redis instance,

00:22:51.410 --> 00:22:55.200
you may not be able
to do that directly.

00:22:55.200 --> 00:22:59.120
So let's look at connecting
to the Redis instance.

00:22:59.120 --> 00:23:00.970
Like I said, Redis is
protocol-compatible.

00:23:00.970 --> 00:23:03.920
So I'm just going to run a
quick standard Redis benchmark

00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:05.400
and see if that works.

00:23:05.400 --> 00:23:08.200
And as you can see, the
use open source Redis,

00:23:08.200 --> 00:23:12.000
so any of the tools that you
use with open source Redis

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:13.130
works great.

00:23:13.130 --> 00:23:14.840
In this particular
case, I'm just

00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:17.030
running a Redis
benchmark that basically

00:23:17.030 --> 00:23:19.530
does a get set
[? push and a pop. ?]

00:23:19.530 --> 00:23:22.030
As you can see, we get pretty
good performance when you are

00:23:22.030 --> 00:23:24.569
using a Memorystore instance.

00:23:24.569 --> 00:23:26.360
Now the interesting
question is, how do you

00:23:26.360 --> 00:23:29.300
monitor the instance?

00:23:29.300 --> 00:23:32.050
As I mentioned earlier,
we have integrated

00:23:32.050 --> 00:23:33.830
with Stackdriver monitoring.

00:23:33.830 --> 00:23:35.870
So to go get to
Stackdriver, let me quickly

00:23:35.870 --> 00:23:37.110
show you how to do that.

00:23:37.110 --> 00:23:40.820
So if you want to get to
Stackdriver and see the Redis

00:23:40.820 --> 00:23:43.550
instance, you basically just
have to go onto Stackdriver

00:23:43.550 --> 00:23:45.260
and click on monitoring.

00:23:45.260 --> 00:23:48.410
And if you launched
Stackdriver monitoring,

00:23:48.410 --> 00:23:52.460
you can get to the
Redis dashboard.

00:23:52.460 --> 00:23:54.620
So when you look at the
dashboard, as you can see,

00:23:54.620 --> 00:23:56.420
we expose a whole
bunch of metrics

00:23:56.420 --> 00:24:00.980
that makes it very, very easy
for you to monitor your Redis

00:24:00.980 --> 00:24:01.942
instance.

00:24:01.942 --> 00:24:04.400
So in this particular case,
you can see the average network

00:24:04.400 --> 00:24:04.940
throughput.

00:24:04.940 --> 00:24:06.660
You can see the CPU utilization.

00:24:11.350 --> 00:24:13.990
You can see the average
commands per second.

00:24:13.990 --> 00:24:16.489
You can see the [INAUDIBLE]
percentile latency.

00:24:16.489 --> 00:24:18.280
So there's a lot of
things that we actually

00:24:18.280 --> 00:24:20.030
expose as part of Stackdriver.

00:24:20.030 --> 00:24:23.900
So let's take a look at what all
the metrics are that we expose.

00:24:23.900 --> 00:24:27.010
So to see the metrics, what
you can do is go to Resources,

00:24:27.010 --> 00:24:32.080
and go to Metrics Explorer, and
just type in, redis.googleapis.

00:24:32.080 --> 00:24:35.260
And you will see the
list of all the metrics

00:24:35.260 --> 00:24:38.780
that are currently published
for you to monitor the Redis

00:24:38.780 --> 00:24:39.280
instance.

00:24:43.530 --> 00:24:49.540
So as I showed
you, it's very easy

00:24:49.540 --> 00:24:51.940
to go deploy a Redis instance.

00:24:51.940 --> 00:24:55.540
It's protocol-compatible, so
that means any of the tools

00:24:55.540 --> 00:24:58.160
will work that you're
currently using.

00:24:58.160 --> 00:25:01.570
So if you're currently using
a [? self-deport ?] Redis

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:07.960
instance, and your applications
are using Redis for whatever

00:25:07.960 --> 00:25:11.585
reason, it is very, very easy
to switch to a Memorystore

00:25:11.585 --> 00:25:12.085
instance.

00:25:14.740 --> 00:25:18.310
And finally, I touched
upon some of the features

00:25:18.310 --> 00:25:19.580
around monitoring.

00:25:19.580 --> 00:25:23.020
So if you want to be able to
manage the Redis instance,

00:25:23.020 --> 00:25:24.580
it's very, very
easy to manage it,

00:25:24.580 --> 00:25:28.000
because we automatically
provide the monitoring for you

00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:30.430
so that you don't have to
integrate with any third party

00:25:30.430 --> 00:25:32.440
tools to do the monitoring.

00:25:32.440 --> 00:25:35.395
Like I mentioned, we take care
of the management of the Redis

00:25:35.395 --> 00:25:37.990
instance so that you
don't have to worry about

00:25:37.990 --> 00:25:39.580
patch management, et cetera.

00:25:39.580 --> 00:25:42.340
All you have to worry about is
just writing your application

00:25:42.340 --> 00:25:46.510
and using Redis for
what it is meant for.

00:25:50.560 --> 00:25:57.190
So that's pretty much the
Cloud Memorystore product.

00:25:57.190 --> 00:25:59.020
It's currently in beta.

00:25:59.020 --> 00:26:02.840
We are planning to
go GA pretty soon.

00:26:02.840 --> 00:26:06.080
But if you are interested
in testing the product,

00:26:06.080 --> 00:26:08.890
you can do that using
free credits that

00:26:08.890 --> 00:26:12.760
are available with the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:26:12.760 --> 00:26:17.290
And if you want to have more
information about Memorystore,

00:26:17.290 --> 00:26:20.910
you can also go to
Google.com/memorystore,

00:26:20.910 --> 00:26:24.670
where we have the documentation
and more information about

00:26:24.670 --> 00:26:26.360
the service.

00:26:26.360 --> 00:26:27.880
Thank you very much.

00:26:27.880 --> 00:27:15.407
Now we are going
to take the Q&amp;A.

00:27:15.407 --> 00:27:17.740
OK, so we already got a few
questions from the audience.

00:27:17.740 --> 00:27:19.150
I'm just going to
quickly address

00:27:19.150 --> 00:27:22.370
some of the questions
that are showing up here.

00:27:22.370 --> 00:27:24.040
So the first
question is, will you

00:27:24.040 --> 00:27:28.150
be providing support
for Redis 4.0?

00:27:28.150 --> 00:27:28.690
Yes.

00:27:28.690 --> 00:27:31.090
Like I mentioned,
currently in beta, we

00:27:31.090 --> 00:27:33.490
are only supporting
3.2, but we plan

00:27:33.490 --> 00:27:37.210
to provide support for
Redis 4.0 pretty soon.

00:27:37.210 --> 00:27:40.540
We don't have exact timeline,
but soon after GA, we

00:27:40.540 --> 00:27:45.640
expect to be able to provide
support for Redis 4.0.

00:27:45.640 --> 00:27:48.880
The other question is, I see
that the current limit is

00:27:48.880 --> 00:27:49.810
300 GB.

00:27:49.810 --> 00:27:53.330
My application requires close
to a terabyte of Redis data.

00:27:53.330 --> 00:27:54.670
What are my options?

00:27:54.670 --> 00:27:56.440
That's a very good question.

00:27:56.440 --> 00:27:58.900
So like I mentioned, the
basic and standard tier

00:27:58.900 --> 00:28:01.200
are essentially
single instance Redis.

00:28:01.200 --> 00:28:04.180
So what that means is that
there is only one master,

00:28:04.180 --> 00:28:10.610
and it does not scale out
Redis cluster solution.

00:28:10.610 --> 00:28:13.270
So in this particular
case, one option

00:28:13.270 --> 00:28:16.390
is to basically deploy
multiple instances of Redis

00:28:16.390 --> 00:28:17.860
and do a client-side sharding.

00:28:17.860 --> 00:28:20.030
That's one way to do it.

00:28:20.030 --> 00:28:22.900
The other thing that
we are working on

00:28:22.900 --> 00:28:24.340
is obviously Redis cluster.

00:28:24.340 --> 00:28:26.590
Again, this is in our
roadmap, and we are actually

00:28:26.590 --> 00:28:29.350
working towards providing
a Redis cluster.

00:28:29.350 --> 00:28:34.120
But until then, the limit
is going to be 300 GB.

00:28:34.120 --> 00:28:36.160
Is persistence supported
in the current release?

00:28:36.160 --> 00:28:37.130
Very good question.

00:28:37.130 --> 00:28:41.330
So this is something that
currently is not supported.

00:28:41.330 --> 00:28:44.650
So let me clarify what we--

00:28:44.650 --> 00:28:46.710
let me clarify
persistence a little bit.

00:28:46.710 --> 00:28:49.640
So there is both-- there is RDB,
and then there is AOF, at least

00:28:49.640 --> 00:28:51.700
in 3.2.

00:28:51.700 --> 00:28:55.370
So we plan to provide RDB
snapshots very quickly,

00:28:55.370 --> 00:28:58.750
where you can take RDB
backups to a GCS bucket.

00:28:58.750 --> 00:29:00.940
AOF is something that
we are working on,

00:29:00.940 --> 00:29:02.780
and we should have
that pretty soon.

00:29:02.780 --> 00:29:05.330
But currently, neither
basic or standard tier

00:29:05.330 --> 00:29:07.390
provides AOF [? persistence. ?]

00:29:07.390 --> 00:29:09.820
And finally, when is
the product going GA?

00:29:09.820 --> 00:29:13.580
We expect the product to go
GA by September of this year.

00:29:13.580 --> 00:29:15.370
So we are actively
working towards that,

00:29:15.370 --> 00:29:19.690
and we hope to have that
released in September.

00:29:19.690 --> 00:29:23.147
So that's all the
questions that I have.

00:29:23.147 --> 00:29:23.980
Thank you very much.

00:29:23.980 --> 00:29:26.260
Thank you for
attending the webinar.

00:29:26.260 --> 00:29:27.640
Have a great day.

00:29:27.640 --> 00:29:31.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

